SM(610,{"n":"Revelation 2.1-17","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Revelation 2.1-17.mp3","d":3620.9,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.64,"Okay, so we'll get started when we get started here."],[23.92,"Uh in uh just a moment, we'll be picking up in uh the Apocalypse of St."],[31.36,"John, uh, also known as the Book of Revelation, also known as the Revelation of St."],[36.48,"John. Uh chapter 2, uh, verse 1."],[41.76,"So uh we went through chapter 1 last time."],[45.28,"That was sort of the beginning of St."],[48.4,"John's vision. Uh he was in worship in whatever worship space he had."],[53.52,"We know this is sort of a cave uh on the island of Patmos."],[57.36,"We know it's a cave because you could go there and see the cave."],[64.12,"This was in a cave."],[65.0,"There was a salt mine on the island of Patmos."],[67.16,"That's where St."],[68.28,"John was exiled to."],[72.36,"And while he's there in the worship space, he has this vision where Christ appears to him in glory."],[81.32,"And he is staying there with seven lampstands."],[86.04,"He has seven stars that are the angels of these seven churches."],[89.56,"The seven lampstands represent the seven churches."],[93.08,"So these are seven churches."],[94.6,"It's Ephesus, city of Ephesus of the area around Ephesus, which is these are the communities, the Christian communities in these cities that uh Sa Johnint. regularly ministered to"],[108.0,"And we talked about how we have these letters to the seven churches now here for the next couple of chapters, next few chapters."],[117.84,"And how originally, when this was sent out, the rest of the text, which is Saint John's Vision, would have been sent a copy to each of these seven churches with sort of a cover letter for that church."],[134.93,"And then once it circulated beyond those seven churches, the book, they compiled all seven of the letters."],[142.39,"Into sort of the form we have now."],[145.44,"And so what we're going to see here is then at the the beginning of vision, Christ is gonna dictate these letters to Saint John."],[153.08,"Saint John is sort of taking down these letters."],[155.88,"Um these aren't really epistles of Saint John to this or that church, because he's taking dictation."],[165.85,"These are epistles of Christ to these churches."],[171.83,"They come sort of directly from him."],[174.09,"So this is one of those uh that most most of the Bible, we say is inspired by God."],[184.95,"That where we encounter Christ through it."],[188.01,"But it's written by a human author."],[190.59,"And when you read, especially the New Testament, the Old Testament's been edited a lot by the time it gets to us, so it's been smoothed out a little bit."],[198.67,"But especially the New Testament, you could very much tell different authors by their style of Greek and that kind of thing."],[206.27,"So there's this like human element."],[208.91,"This is one of the rare places where this is sort of Christ speaking directly."],[213.65,"Where he says to the human, like Saint Paul was not like taking dictation when he wrote to the church in Rome."],[220.19,"He was letting writing a letter as Saint Paul to the church in Rome."],[223.71,"We believe that the Holy Spirit works through that letter to bring us to Christ."],[228.19,"Whereas this is Christ saying, John, write this down."],[231.15,"saying, Like exactly what I'm"],[233.23,"John. So there's not really Saint in this"],[240.0,"Now that makes it different than the rest of Revelation too."],[245.34,"Because the rest of it is going to be St."],[247.42,"John writing down the things that he saw for the most part."],[250.86,"There's a couple other pieces like this, but most of it is going to be him writing down, like we saw in chapter one, him writing down the things that he saw."],[259.34,"And so it's sort of processed through St."],[261.58,"John's mind. Um, and so we're going to see as we go forward some of the same patterns of his writing that we saw in his epistles and in his gospel."],[275.43,"Um the epistles will probably be more fresh since we went over St."],[279.27,"John's gospel like five years ago."],[281.51,"But the um but we'll see some of those same patterns, as we go forward."],[287.13,"But so it's important to sort of recognize the difference here in these letters, uh, which are words direct, sort of direct from Christ."],[296.25,"Um that said, one of the reasons why I make that point and the point about the cover letters is that as I think I mentioned either in the introduction or last time, uh, there are certain folks, uh this is a the the Puritans were a big fan of this, uh, who try to turn the letters into like seven ages of the church."],[322.21,"Like the first letter is like, you know, they put attach years to it."],[326.79,"Try and map it onto church history and stuff."],[329.91,"And uh that means that like the sixth and seventh churches received letters that were completely irrelevant to them because it was stuff directed at people thousands of years in the future."],[341.83,"Which doesn't make a lot of sense."],[343.52,"So that's not what's going on here."],[346.4,"Sorry, Jonathan Edwards, at all."],[349.76,"Uh that doesn't work."],[352.72,"Um and that counts as a sorry Calvinist because Jonathan Edwards was indeed a Calvinist."],[359.36,"Um, all that said, by way of catch up, um, we'll go ahead now, unless there's any other comments or questions or funny limericks that anybody heard today."],[383.08,"To the angel of the church of Ephesus."],[386.89,"We'll pause right there."],[389.61,"I won't do this every time at the beginning of each letter because it's gonna say the exact same words just with a different city."],[395.29,"But for the first one, um remember, and this is sort of review because we talked about this at the end last time when we talked about what it means to have the angels of these churches."],[406.59,"And we talked about how a group."],[410.41,"A collective group has a life."],[413.79,"Has a spirit."],[415.16,"That's beyond just the individual lives of the individual people."],[419.02,"So, like our parish here has a life."],[422.91,"Together, that we all share, and that's beyond just my life plus Arthur's life plus your life plus your life."],[429.57,"There's a life that we share together."],[432.15,"And so that life, that body has a spirit."],[436.35,"And so these letters are all going to be addressed, not to the people of the Christian community in this city, but they're addressed to the angel, they're addressed to that spirit, they're addressed to the collective life of that community."],[453.05,"And we're going to see that they address the collective life of that community."],[460.25,"So these really are letters to the churches, not letters to the individual people in the churches."],[477.29,"In the modern world."],[481.01,"There's something going wrong in the church or in our town, or in our country, or in any group that we're a part of, our first instinct is to try to render ourselves innocent of whatever it is."],[494.85,"Yeah, but that's not me, that's the rest of those people."],[498.15,"I'm one of the good I don't have anything to do with that."],[501.63,"But if there's if there's something wrong, with the whole, then that's something that has to be addressed by every member of the whole."],[514.54,"So if you get gangrene in your left big toe, you can't just be like, well, that's my left big toe."],[520.78,"I'm not that worried about it."],[523.12,"Because the rest of my body's fine."],[525.54,"Even if it's just your right, because that will kill your whole body if you don't do something about it."],[532.79,"It's something that has to be addressed by the whole."],[537.95,"And so as we read these, you're gonna see, well, really, was every person in the church there doing XYZ?"],[546.21,"Was every person that's not the point?"],[549.99,"That's not the point."],[550.87,"The problem is affecting everybody."],[552.71,"And so the problem needs to be addressed as a group by everybody."],[561.03,"So to the angel of the church of Ephesus, so we're starting with the central big city, the main, sort of the mother church, of these communities."],[572.17,"These things says he who holds the seven stars in his right hand, who walks in the midst of the seven golden lampstands."],[579.93,"Okay, so we're also going to see in each of these, there's going to be an identifier for Christ that's going to be based on parts of things we saw in chapter one, the way he was described."],[592.73,"And we're going to see as we go along, there's going to be a few other little things tossed into the mix, too, that we're going to see later on in the visions of the book."],[601.45,"But so this is pretty much how we just saw him described at the end of chapter one."],[606.11,"He's standing there, he's got the seven stars in his hand that are the seven angels."],[609.55,"He's standing among the seven lampstands that are the seven churches."],[615.47,"Verse 2 I know your works, your label, labor, your patience, and that you cannot bear those who are evil."],[623.79,"And you have tested those who say they are apostles and are not, and have found them liars"],[630.95,"So so far, so good."],[633.16,"And remember, these works he's talking about are not like individual people."],[639.18,"This is how the whole is functioning, how the whole community is functioning."],[643.82,"That the community is working together, the community is being patient and suffering."],[648.8,"That they cannot bear those who are evil."],[650.74,"So if someone in that community has fallen into sin."],[654.92,"The'yre either bringing them to repentance or they're out."],[661.48,"And that when someone shows up claiming to be a teacher, claiming to be apostle, remember apostle means someone sent."],[666.76,"They're testing them"],[667.76,"So they claim they're there."],[669.36,"God sent them there."],[672.36,"And the ones who are phonies, they're finding out that they're phonies and they're casting them out."],[677.88,"So all so far, so good."],[682.2,"Verse 3, you have persevered and have patience, and have labored for my namesake, and have not become weary."],[691.48,"So this is remember St."],[694.12,"John, he circles around and around and around."],[696.5,"So again, you're patient in suffering."],[700.98,"And you keep working hard."],[703.3,"You don't get tired."],[704.58,"You're not taking breaks."],[706.02,"You're not resting on your laurels."],[708.98,"Verse 4. Nevertheless. uh oh, it takes a turn"],[713.82,"I have this against you that you have left your first love."],[719.9,"Okay. Um, this isn't talking about like you abandoned your first wife."],[726.7,"This is talking about particular."],[728.92,"But there was a state they were in when they first came to know Christ."],[737.64,"When they first came into the community, when they first formed this community together."],[741.56,"There was this love that they had for each other."],[744.93,"And there was this beauty and this simplicity about it."],[748.13,"And they've kept working hard, they've kept doing all these other things, but somewhere along the line."],[754.78,"That love sort of slipped away."],[759.36,"It sort of slipped away."],[761.36,"And this letter and several of the other letters have in the background the analogy of the church as the bride of Christ."],[774.85,"Um, which of course is a development of what we see all through the prophets in the Old Testament of Israel as the bride of the God of Israel."],[784.31,"Of Yahweh. So part of that analogy is there."],[791.52,"It's sort of, you know, that sort of newlyweds honeymoon state."],[796.48,"You're still doing good"],[797.7,"You say, look, you're still faithful."],[802.32,"You're still doing the right things."],[804.56,"But somewhere along the line that the honeymoon ended."],[810.06,"That sort of love we had has kind of gotten cold."],[817.15,"Verse 5, remember therefore, from where you have fallen, repent and do the first works, or else I will come to you quickly and remove your lampstand from its place, unless you repent."],[830.71,"Okay. So remember, repent, doesn't always just mean you've been bad, say you're sorry."],[838.55,"Be healed, be transformed"],[839.92,"Repent means change."],[844.62,"So he's saying you need to go back to the way things were."],[850.08,"Now, how can that be if they're doing all these good things?"],[853.44,"Remember what St."],[854.4,"Paul says in 1 Corinthians 13."],[856.8,"He gives that big long list."],[858.5,"If I could speak with the tongue of men and angels, and I could do all things through faith, I could do it."],[862.82,"On and on and on."],[863.7,"He says, but I don't have love, then I'm nothing."],[868.46,"This is that same kind of idea."],[870.94,"You need to get back."],[872.56,"You need to get that love back."],[874.24,"Because if all these things you're doing aren't motivated by love, if they've just sort of been institutionalized in your community."],[879.92,"you If you break them and don't want to be out here's repent."],[881.1,"These are the rules."],[881.9,"If you break them, you're out."],[888.0,"And these things all become just sort of hardened."],[890.4,"But that can become going through the motions."],[892.96,"Like Israel offering the sacrifices, but not keeping the commandments."],[898.85,"So that's what this is getting at."],[901.65,"You need to go back to that love."],[904.13,"And then what's the danger?"],[907.01,"The danger is if they don't do that, he's gonna come and remove the lampstand."],[915.21,"That symbol. We talked about the lampstand."],[918.27,"This is the seven branch candle stand, like the one that was in the uh tabernacle. the one that was in the temple in Jerusalem"],[926.47,"Uh if you've ever seen the Arch of Titus, the scene commemorating his destruction of the temple in Jerusalem is what?"],[935.27,"Them carrying away the lampstand."],[941.61,"And so this is referring back."],[944.49,"That's not narrated in the New Testament."],[948.33,"But what's narrated in the Old Testament is when the Babylonians destroyed the first temple."],[953.45,"And leading up to that, the prophets were saying the same thing."],[956.81,"They were using that language of Israel as God's spouse."],[961.85,"They were coming to them and saying, Look, you're going through the motions, you're offering the sacrifices, and you're doing this stuff"],[968.29,"But God said, Your heart is far from me."],[973.35,"The love isn't there."],[974.87,"And what ended up happening?"],[978.07,"That temple was destroyed."],[980.09,"So that's what he's talking about."],[981.93,"So this church in Ephesus is a temple."],[985.01,"But you can go down this same route."],[989.35,"That Israel went down."],[991.19,"That Judea went down."],[993.43,"And it'll have the same kind of consequences."],[998.23,"If you do."],[1001.99,"Verse 6, but this you have that you hate the deeds of the Nikolaitans, which I also hate."],[1007.91,"So, well, you got this going for you."],[1009.51,"You don't like them Nicolaitans"],[1014.35,"So traditionally, um there's no other identifier."],[1019.79,"I mean, this is the last book written in the New Testament."],[1022.99,"It's written around, not probably written around 96, remember, because St."],[1027.39,"Irenaeus told us that St."],[1029.47,"John had this vision in 95."],[1031.95,"Um. So we don't have other stuff about the Nicolaitans."],[1037.71,"That's why I say traditionally."],[1039.87,"Um you find this interpretation in the church fathers, but it's not in like the church fathers immediately after this."],[1047.79,"Like you don't find the second century church fathers talking about the Nicolaitans."],[1052.59,"You find like fourth century church fathers and fifth century and sixth century church fathers where they talk about this part of the book of Revelation explaining who the Nicolaitans are."],[1063.51,"So that's what I mean by traditionally, to be real specific about it."],[1068.15,"And they identify it as coming from I mean Nicolaitan literally means the party of Nicholas."],[1074.87,"Uh and uh this is obviously not Saint Nicholas in the fourth century."],[1079.67,"Uh this is another Nicholas, this is Nicholas the the deacon, traditionally."],[1085.91,"So in the book of Acts, they appoint seven deacons."],[1090.71,"Remember, St. Stephen's one of them who gets martyred, Saint Philip is one of them."],[1095.43,"Um he has daughters who are prophetesses."],[1098.71,"Uh and one of them is Nicholas, Nicolaus, literally."],[1104.35,"And so traditionally, that Nicholas, that Nicolaus, who was made a deacon by the apostles, went into business for himself, shall we say."],[1116.75,"And started his own kind of sect with his own beliefs."],[1122.99,"And tried to use sort of his authority as a deacon appointed by the apostles to uh command authority in churches and sway people into joining his movement."],[1137.31,"And so this would just contextually, regardless of what we do with that identification, this would be one of those false apostles and false teachers that were discussed earlier in this letter to Ephesus, who they had sort of tested and decided was false."],[1155.13,"So one of those were was the Nicolaitans."],[1159.34,"And uh notice it doesn't say you hate the Nicolaitans, who I also hate."],[1169.42,"It says you hate the works of the Nicolaitans."],[1173.34,"You hate what they're doing, you hate what they're doing to the church and in the churches."],[1180.91,"Which Christ also hates what they're doing."],[1184.47,"That is an important distinction."],[1189.41,"Because people who think that God hates heretics kill them."],[1196.05,"Whereas people who who think God hates the works of heretics try to bring them to repentance."],[1201.57,"A very different approach."],[1207.81,"Verse 7, he who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches."],[1213.73,"That's a very, how many times does Christ say that in the Gospels?"],[1218.69,"Right between his parables, he who has an ear to hear, let him hear."],[1222.77,"What the Spirit says to the churches, to him who overcomes, I will give to eat from the tree of life, which is in the midst of the paradise of God."],[1236.01,"So we're gonna see in several of these letters that the person, the quote-unquote in our modern parlance, saved person."],[1247.71,"The person who does what is right, the person who does what Christ is asking of them is the one who overcomes."],[1253.71,"Literally, sort of the one who conquers."],[1257.95,"This has a lot of resonance in the Roman world."],[1264.27,"Because that's what imperat imperator means."],[1267.41,"Which we translate as emperor."],[1271.57,"You couldn't get away with calling yourself a king in Rome."],[1274.77,"The word king had a bad rap."],[1275.49,"From the tyrants"],[1280.17,"So it was imperator, the conqueror, the victorious general."],[1286.54,"But Christ is here using that to describe the uh the Christian who is faithful to the end of their life."],[1296.7,"That's the one who's victorious in the battle."],[1300.19,"And victorious in war."],[1301.79,"The one who's crowned."],[1304.35,"That's where that crowning language comes from."],[1308.35,"So we're gonna see that that's over the one who overcomes."],[1313.17,"So that means what he's calling on them to do. when he says, return to your first love, is not about being sentimental"],[1325.75,"It's not about rekindling romance."],[1329.8,"Getting touchy-feely. Getting that tingle on your spine when you hear the hymn at the beginning of the church service."],[1336.35,"That's not what he's talking about."],[1339.39,"What he's calling them to do is going to be a struggle."],[1344.27,"They're going to have to struggle, they're going to have to fight to get back to where they were."],[1350.21,"But to the one who does that, the one who enters into that struggle and comes out the other end victorious, what are they going to receive?"],[1357.01,"They're going to get to eat from the tree of life in paradise."],[1359.97,"Well, what was that?"],[1362.17,"The expul God expelled humanity from paradise so they wouldn't eat from the tree of life while they were evil, because that would make them immortal."],[1374.89,"And if you're immortal and evil, that's a demon."],[1380.33,"God didn't want humans to become demons, so he casts about into this world and gave humans this life in order to repent."],[1388.65,"So we wouldn't end up like the demons if we take that opportunity."],[1392.43,"And so Christ is saying to the one who overcomes, he's going to give immortality."],[1398.99,"He's going to give eternal life"],[1402.59,"You're going to be able to do what they were not allowed to do."],[1405.39,"One down, six to go"],[1408.39,"So that's letter one."],[1415.19,"Verse 8. And to the angel of the church in Smyrna."],[1419.64,"These things says the first and the last who was dead and came to life."],[1428.44,"For Christ. I know your works, tribulation, and poverty, but you are rich."],[1439.0,"We'll pause there."],[1441.56,"So he knows their works, he knows what they do."],[1446.52,"He knows their tribulation."],[1447.8,"And he also knows their poverty"],[1447.92,"He knows the suffering they've gone through."],[1453.52,"But then he clarifies, but you're rich."],[1457.68,"Meaning, in terms of money, in terms of how they view themselves, they're rich, but they're actually poor."],[1466.16,"That's gonna get developed some more here."],[1469.76,"Wait a minute."],[1470.64,"So they are in a material sense rich."],[1475.68,"Poverty is spirit."],[1478.85,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."],[1485.01,"And I know the blasphemy of those who say they are Jews and are not, but are a synagogue of Satan."],[1494.45,"That's pretty blunt"],[1497.13,"So what this means is that Smyrna at this point, uh there's we talked about how um Christianity and Judaism are not separate religions."],[1511.15,"Even in ninety five."],[1512.8,"Um so you have synagogues and gatherings of Christians."],[1520.98,"Who are of Jewish backgrounds and non Jewish backgrounds."],[1524.32,"And you have obviously still gatherings of Jewish people who are not Christians."],[1529.45,"Who do not accept that Jesus is the Messiah."],[1533.37,"And so this group is not being called the synagogue of Satan just because they haven't accepted Christ."],[1544.27,"Because it's saying he knows of the blasphemies of those who say they're Jews but aren't."],[1550.11,"Or the synagogue of Satan."],[1551.47,"This is a group that is sort of deliberately, we saw this a lot in the book of Acts."],[1556.53,"Deliberately stirring up trouble for the Christian community, probably with the Roman authorities."],[1563.97,"And we talked about how it varied a lot from place to place, what relations were like between Christian Jewish communities and non-Christian Jewish communities."],[1575.73,"Some places they were mixed together, all going to the same synagogues."],[1580.05,"es. Other places there was more tension from both sid"],[1587.19,"From both sides."],[1588.79,"But where that sort of crossed the line would be when you get like the civil magistrate involved, when you get the Roman authorities involved to try to disadvantage, persecute, imprison, whatever the other group, who is your rival."],[1607.77,"And so that's what this is indicating."],[1609.13,"That in Smyrna, at this time, there's a non-Christian Jewish community."],[1613.17,"That the problem is not that there are non-Christian, because there were non-Christian Jewish communities in every one of these cities."],[1620.1,"They're not singled out in every city."],[1622.5,"This one, this is a group of people who are actively using the Roman authorities to persecute."],[1632.0,"The Christian community."],[1633.12,"So he's saying he knows about this."],[1638.8,"Verse 10 do not fear any of those things which you are about to suffer."],[1643.68,"Indeed, the devil is about to throw some of you into prison that you may be tested, and you will have tribulation ten days."],[1652.16,"Be faithful unto death, and I will give you the crown of life."],[1661.2,"So that's pretty clear."],[1663.14,"The biggest concern for them is not that they need to do something they're not doing, they need to stop doing something they are doing."],[1667.62,"they need And so what're going to to do, their lives in the near future are going to be consumed with remaining faithful despite that persecution."],[1670.46,"There is this external persecution coming."],[1682.78,"Because all of this persecution is in the form of all you have to do is stop being a Christian."],[1688.78,"And all of this goes away."],[1699.52,"Just have to say a couple of words."],[1701.66,"And so Christ is saying the one who is faithful until death."],[1708.55,"And some of those deaths are going to be in prison."],[1713.03,"Some of those deaths are going to be after torture."],[1716.07,"Some of those deaths might be executions."],[1719.13,"And some of those, some of those might be deaths 50 years from then, and they're going to need to remain faithful that whole time."],[1725.85,"And there's going to be good times and bad times."],[1728.99,"But the one who endures until their death, they receive the crown of life."],[1737.63,"So that crowning, conquering, victory language again."],[1741.87,"We're going to see it again here in verse 11."],[1743.71,"He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches."],[1748.91,"He who overcomes shall not be hurt by the second death."],[1756.31,"So there is here a first death, there is a second death."],[1762.68,"Everyone is going to experience the first death."],[1766.28,"Every human at least."],[1770.92,"Fair enough. Exceptions that prove the rule."],[1774.84,"The rest of us."],[1775.96,"We're going to all die once."],[1781.08,"But remember that language that's applied to Christ even in chapter 1."],[1785.77,"Christ died. He will never die again."],[1790.01,"And so this is the same promise being made to the one who's faithful to Christ."],[1796.57,"So this is referring to the eternal state."],[1798.09,"The eternal state"],[1804.1,"And the second death, or eternal death, is one of the many metaphors that's used to describe the bad way that can end up."],[1815.62,"None of which are literal."],[1817.14,"They can't be literal because they contradict each other."],[1820.66,"So if you've got an eternal fire, you can't be in eternal darkness at the same time, because fires give off light."],[1829.28,"So we have different metaphors for this."],[1832.8,"All informing us that this is really bad and we don't want it to happen to us."],[1837.74,"None of them telling us that it for certain is going to happen to anyone in particular."],[1844.32,"But it's there as a possibility."],[1847.76,"There is a possibility."],[1849.68,"A potential future for us."],[1853.76,"And so, in light of that, the one who overcomes, the one who's victorious, the one who remains faithful doesn't have to worry about that."],[1863.3,"That is removed from the realm of future possibility."],[1870.18,"I will briefly say."],[1872.82,"So there are some folks out there."],[1876.5,"I don't know if they still call it this."],[1878.42,"I'm sure they've come up with some new term, probably with an extra eight syllables."],[1882.82,"Used to be called annihilationism."],[1886.3,"Where they believe that people who find eternal life in Christ have eternal life."],[1894.06,"And then people who don't just cease to exist."],[1898.94,"Like at the last judgment."],[1900.54,"Or when they die, or somewhere in between."],[1903.52,"There's some point at which they just sort of cease to exist."],[1907.84,"The reason I bring this up here is that they take this second death language and say, well, see, second death."],[1914.82,"So they just they just die."],[1916.92,"Here's the problem with that argument."],[1919.56,"That argument assumes that the word death means cease to exist."],[1926.2,"Does the first death mean you cease to exist?"],[1927.0,"death mean you cease to exist So why would the second?"],[1930.16,"No. They've got to equivocate on the word death."],[1936.58,"Well, how can you say death doesn't mean you cease to exist?"],[1941.54,"Your body's gone."],[1943.22,"If you have an immortal soul, then you're not going to be able to do that."],[1946.71,"I mean you don't cease to exist."],[1948.15,"You're just your soul is separated from your body."],[1950.61,"And then the resurrection they're restored to each other, and then but you don't cease to exist."],[1955.91,"So you have to equivocate on the word death."],[1958.07,"You have to say, well, death in the case of the first death means your soul is separated from your body, and death in the case of the second death means you completely cease to exist."],[1967.53,"And by calling it the second death, you're doing the opposite of saying that these are two different things."],[1970.81,"ing You're say that they're in some way similar."],[1978.23,"So it doesn't make sense then to say the word death means two different things in the same comparison."],[1986.0,"Because then it's not a comparison."],[1990.64,"If I say, you know, my car is similar to my dog, you're gonna be confused."],[1998.37,"If I say my 2000 Ford Escape is similar to my neighbor's 2001 Ford Escape."],[2005.99,"You're gonna say, ah, yes, those two things would be similar."],[2010.31,"I'd be making a sensible comparison."],[2013.63,"But. that was just an aside, just because sometimes people bring that up"],[2018.59,"I'm heading off a future email to ancient faith asking me about that."],[2025.15,"Verse 12. And to the angel of the church in Pergamos."],[2032.0,"These things says he who has the sharp two edged sword."],[2035.6,"Again, from the first chapter."],[2037.22,"That was coming out of his mouth."],[2038.74,"And we talked about how you're not supposed to literally try to draw that."],[2045.42,"I know your works and where you dwell, where Satan's throne is, and you hold fast to my name and did not deny my faith, even in the days in which Antipas was my faithful martyr, who was killed among you where Satan dwells."],[2063.14,"Okay, so this is uh written to the church at Pergamos, also known as Pergamum, same place."],[2072.26,"Um Latin name, Greek name, uh for the city."],[2077.7,"And this throne of Satan that's being referred to is a literal physical object that you could go see in Berlin."],[2087.38,"Um because uh at the Acropolis, the high point of the city of Pergamos, there was a great altar to Zeus, uh, that uh uh depicts in statuary the Gigantomachi, the Olympian gods battling the giants."],[2110.45,"And it is a massive affair."],[2113.25,"This was up at the highest point over the city."],[2115.73,"They were offering pagan sacrifices on it to Zeus, king of the, you know, Jupiter, king of the gods, continuously."],[2123.17,"So there's smoke rising up for it."],[2124.79,"The whole city is in the shadow of this pagan gigantic pagan altar."],[2130.81,"Celebrating sort of the glory of this pagan god."],[2134.09,"Um so why is Zeus in particular associated with Satan?"],[2138.25,"Other than, you know, the gods of the nations or demons."],[2141.05,"This guy's the king, so he must be the devil."],[2145.45,"That is part of it."],[2147.11,"But that's not the only part of it."],[2148.95,"It's that when you find the devil described in the Old Testament, like in Isaiah and Ezekiel, there are these sort of poetic narrations of the fall of the devil."],[2163.35,"And in those, one of them's debatable, but in uh pretty much in both of those, he's compared to Baal."],[2174.73,"The story of Baal in the Baal cycle."],[2177.37,"The Phoenician god Baal, who, but who was worshipped throughout Canaan and Palestine."],[2185.85,"So the pagan god uh Baal, Baal is a storm god who in the Baal cycle stages a revolution with his fellow gods against uh the most high god at the time and defeats him and makes himself the most high god and his dad El."],[2214.5,"So uh that's that story is what's called the sex succession myth because it's repeated over and over again in ancient Near Eastern cultures."],[2223.38,"There's some version of it."],[2224.5,"Marduk does it."],[2226.42,"Um and famously, Zeus does it."],[2230.36,"Zeus kills his father."],[2232.28,"Cronus becomes king of the gods"],[2237.52,"And uh that's kind of payback because Cronus castrated his father, uh Uranus, in order to take over."],[2244.64,"Um so that's how and Zeus is a storm god."],[2250.84,"When um Alexander the Great got to uh what became the city of Antioch in Syria, uh for which our patriarchate is named."],[2264.48,"It's when he got to where that city would end up being."],[2267.4,"Uh there was an ancient shrine there to Baal, and he rededicated it as a shrine to Zeus Boanerges, Zeus the Thunderer."],[2277.92,"Um for several centuries it had been accepted that Zeus and Baal were sort of the same dude."],[2288.14,"We're sort of hypostases of the same god."],[2290.56,"Different versions of the same god."],[2292.56,"Um so it's very natural then if Baal is sort of a picture of the devil."],[2299.7,"So of course, in the devil's own propaganda, he wins his rebellion."],[2303.08,"He wins his his insurrection, and so everyone should worship him."],[2307.93,"Um, and then from the old testament perspective, like, no, you lost."],[2312.09,"That's why your palace is in hell."],[2313.69,"In the Baal cycle, it's interesting."],[2315.21,"Baal, like, he he goes and fights uh goes goes and fights Yom and Nahar, you know, he goes and fights the most high god and totally wins and then descends into the underworld."],[2329.65,"And uh when he gets to the underworld, he fights Moat, the god of death, and again totally wins, but just decides to stay there and build his palace there anyway."],[2338.77,"Um sort of even with built into the bail cycle, there's sort of this really?"],[2347.57,"You won, huh?"],[2349.65,"Okay. Like seems a little odd, but alright."],[2353.57,"Um but and that gets played with then in the old testament."],[2358.13,"Sort of it gets it gets sort of thrown back in the devil."],[2360.85,"So this idea that Zeus is Satan, that's being used here sort of offhandedly was offhand for Jewish people by this point in history."],[2370.57,"Like, yes, these Romans are all worshiping the devil."],[2374.27,"Like and demons, and this is sort of false worship."],[2379.52,"But that reference has this resonance because this is a Christian community that's constantly living in the shadow of that."],[2391.2,"That's what you see when you look up at the skyline is the smoke belching up into the sky."],[2397.65,"And this is a center of imperial power."],[2401.75,"This isn't just the high altar for for Pergamum, this is one of the primary festival altars for the Roman Empire at this time."],[2409.8,"So generals. Major Roman officials would sort of go on pilgrimages there."],[2414.26,"They would go there, especially if they're on their way like to the Eastern Front, go there and offer sacrifices, to Zeus."],[2421.14,"So they're sort of in the middle of the dead center of hostile territory, where they're living."],[2427.4,"And we read here that there's even been this Otipus who's been killed, by the Roman authorities."],[2437.55,"For being a Christian."],[2438.75,"And so they're congratulating, you know, you're living deep in the heart of enemy territory."],[2443.39,"You're facing this persecution."],[2445.49,"Someone's been killed, but you're still staying faithful."],[2452.14,"Verse 14 But I have a few things against you, because you have there those who hold the doctrine of Balaam, who taught Balak to put a stumbling block before the children of Israel, to eat things sacrificed to idols, and to commit sexual immorality."],[2473.02,"So we'll pause there."],[2474.22,"So notice when he says, I have a few things against you, he then talks about people who are in the community."],[2483.02,"Notice again, he's writing to the community, but those people in the community."],[2488.0,"He's not just writing a letter to them, chiding them or telling them to repent."],[2491.61,"It's the community."],[2492.41,"The community needs to deal with this."],[2495.3,"So this is somewhat cryptic."],[2497.64,"Because this is referring to people from the book of Numbers."],[2501.72,"And I know Numbers is everyone's favorite book of the Bible."],[2505.24,"You do daily readings from Numbers, especially the genealogies and the census."],[2510.76,"It's just everybody's favorite stuff."],[2512.84,"But um Balaam, you probably know the story with the donkey."],[2518.66,"That's yeah, that's related to this because Balak is the uh is the king uh of uh Moab who hires Balaam to come and curse Israel."],[2534.15,"Well, that's we're gonna get there in a second."],[2535.75,"Yeah So and and uh Balak is not to be confused with Balak from the First Federation in the first season Star Trek episode, uh the Corbamite maneuver, is also not to be confused with Balok from Raiders of the Lost Ark."],[2559.39,"Takes place much later, even though the Ark of the Covenant's involved."],[2562.75,"Um but so um Balak hired Balaam."],[2570.11,"Now, notice here that's kind of reversed."],[2572.28,"Balaam taught Balak something."],[2575.44,"Because we're moving to a later incident than the story with the donkey."],[2580.52,"Where he tries to curse Israel and fails and ends up blessing them over and over again"],[2587.5,"This is a less funny story."],[2589.34,"That story has the talking donkey."],[2591.6,"You have him going and trying to curse Israel, and then he says a blessing, and they're like, no, you know, it's kind of funny."],[2596.96,"This one's far less funny."],[2598.69,"So this is they get to uh Baal Peor."],[2605.54,"So Paor is a place where there's a high place, meaning there's a hill where Baal, Baal, is worshipped."],[2615.14,"And uh when Israel gets there, uh Balaam, this is where they teaches Balak thing says, okay, look, the reason we had this so much trouble the first time is that their God was protecting them."],[2633.19,"So what you need to do is you need to get them to offend their God."],[2638.55,"And then their God won't protect them anymore."],[2641.51,"And then you can get them."],[2644.76,"So, and the way that he suggested the men of Israel in particular be lured out from under God's protection was that they send."],[2661.32,"So you usually get like priestesses in English Bibles."],[2665.88,"Uh, these are not priestesses, these are what would be more correctly termed shrine prostitutes"],[2673.2,"Um prostitute implies money, and this was more of a religious function."],[2680.72,"Um, so I don't want to go into this too much, but Baal being a storm god also has this element of sending the reins for fertility."],[2692.22,"And so one of the primary ways in which fertility gods like Baal were ritually worshipped in the ancient Near East was the idea of fertilizing the land was enacted with a woman who was at the shrine for this purpose."],[2711.78,"So men who were farmers would come and engage sexually with these shrine workers, and that would enact sort of the fertilization of their soil and their crops to guarantee a good harvest."],[2733.86,"So these women come to Israelite men who are very cooperative and go with them to the shrine, engage in this ritual immorality, also engage in offering sacrifices to Baal."],[2754.88,"And that then sort of wrecks everything."],[2757.72,"A plague comes upon the people."],[2760.52,"So Christ is referring back to that as sort of an example."],[2765.32,"Saying something similar is happening there."],[2770.54,"That there are people being lured and there are people doing the luring, within to get members of the Christian community to participate in the sexual immorality and the uhf pagan sacriices that are going on in the city."],[2793.37,"Because again, this is the big temptation."],[2798.03,"The whole reason people are beinging persecuted, the whole reason Antipas gets killed is that the Christians aren't will to participate in the festivals."],[2806.95,"Aren't willing to participate in these rituals, aren't willing to participate in these sacrifices."],[2811.75,"So if you just go do it, everyone will leave you alone."],[2815.43,"And you can still be a Christian."],[2816.71,"I mean, you can still go to church and stuff."],[2818.31,"I mean, you just, you know, you just do what you have to do to get along."],[2822.06,"And so there are people, not only are people in the community doing that, which is obviously not good, but there are people in the community saying, yeah, I mean, you didn't need to get killed over this."],[2831.98,"Come on, man."],[2833.22,"Like, just go along with it."],[2835.38,"So that's a problem."],[2837.24,"That has to be dealt with by the community."],[2840.3,"Most of them are standing strong. but if you have people making excuses and leading other people, that has to be dealt with"],[2846.66,"So there's that going on, which is not good."],[2851.22,"Verse 15. Thus you also have those who hold the doctrine of the Nicolaitans, which thing I hate."],[2859.56,"Back to them again."],[2860.76,"So they must be in the area."],[2862.86,"Because all these cities are in the same area."],[2865.42,"He says, so some of them have started following the Nicolaitans."],[2871.34,"Notice again, he doesn't say who I hate."],[2876.23,"It's which thing I hate, meaning it's referring back to the doctrine."],[2881.13,"That's hated. Not the people."],[2884.85,"Verse 16, repent, or else I will come to you quickly, and will fight against them with the sword of my mouth."],[2894.05,"Okay. Notice he doesn't say, repent, or I'm going to come kill all of you."],[2901.46,"He says, repent, or I'll come to you quickly, and I will fight against them."],[2907.38,"So judgment is coming for the people who are doing this."],[2914.66,"So we have to differentiate."],[2916.44,"Him writing to the community, and this being a community issue, and innocent people getting punished along with, those who have have been doing the wrong things."],[2927.73,"Remember Abraham with Sodom and Gomorrah trying to sort of haggle with God"],[2934.49,"Well, what if I could find 50 people?"],[2935.77,"What if I could find 45 people?"],[2937.21,"What if I could find 40 people?"],[2939.13,"It's sort of working its way down."],[2943.26,"So, but the command to repent is directed to the whole community."],[2953.34,"So the whole community is to repent."],[2956.06,"Why? For the sake of saving those people from the consequences that will come if they don't change."],[2969.18,"And so that is the opposite, the idea. of kind of communal repentance is the opposite of our natural instinct"],[2978.9,"Our natural instinct is to justify ourselves."],[2981.86,"Our natural instinct, if we were in this city, reading this would be well, I'm certainly not doing that."],[2987.38,"I'm one of the ones who's been suffering because I've been resisting."],[2992.42,"Say, you know what?"],[2993.54,"I think uh I think God should come and take care of those people who aren't doing what I'm doing."],[2998.82,"I kind of resent it."],[3000.24,"Um, this is the opposite of that."],[3004.96,"And we find this idea in a lot of the Desert Fathers and a lot of the monastics."],[3010.82,"Where monastics will repent of other monks' sins."],[3016.18,"As a way of showing solidarity in Christ"],[3023.58,"So if we're one in Christ, and you're my brother in Christ, my sister in Christ, and I see you fall into sin."],[3031.8,"What am I going to want to do?"],[3035.0,"I want you to find salvation."],[3038.44,"And so I'm going to repent, hopefully, with you."],[3054.76,"We all need to get on the same page together."],[3059.26,"And do what we should be doing for the sake of those who are going to perish if they continue in the way that they're going."],[3069.86,"Verse 17: He who has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches."],[3075.06,"To him who overcomes, I will give some of the hidden manna to eat."],[3080.34,"And I will give him a white stone, and on the stone a new name written, which no one knows except him who receives it."],[3092.58,"So that's sort of two parts."],[3094.99,"So the first one, we have that overcome language again."],[3097.71,"The first part gives some of the hidden hidden manna to eat."],[3101.93,"Mana, remember, is the the bread of heaven."],[3106.15,"The bread that comes down from heaven and that is sort of completely nourishing and satisfying."],[3114.44,"Um this is being contrasted with, remember, it mentioned eating food offered to idols."],[3122.26,"Because that's how sacrifices are meals."],[3124.82,"Sacrifices are not about killing things."],[3127.46,"These sacrifices were meals, and the way you participate in it is by eating some of it."],[3133.78,"You go to the feast, they bring in the meat from the animal that's been sacrificed, you all eat it."],[3138.58,"That's how you participate in the worship."],[3140.86,"So they're commanded not to eat, which for a lot of early Christians, as St."],[3145.66,"Paul says when he talks about food offered to idols, means being vegetarians."],[3151.22,"A lot of these cities, like Corinth, like uh Athens, at this time in history, pretty much all the meat that was out for sale in the meat market came from the temples."],[3163.51,"So they're denying themselves food."],[3165.99,"Christ's promise is that he's going to give them the headmanly food that really nourishes."],[3171.21,"And really satisfies over against."],[3173.71,"The sort of demonic food, that's coming from these temples."],[3178.21,"The second part about being given a new name."],[3182.25,"On uh on a white stone that no one else knows."],[3187.94,"So when someone gets a new name, this is something that happens throughout the Bible."],[3193.72,"We've seen a bunch of examples of it."],[3195.34,"Abram becomes Abraham."],[3197.5,"Sarai becomes Sarah."],[3199.52,"Jacob becomes Israel."],[3208.72,"Simon Barjona becomes Simon the Rock Barjona."],[3211.92,"He becomes St."],[3212.56,"Peter. He becomes Petros or Cephas."],[3217.9,"Even a new name."],[3219.02,"Saul, Saul sort of becomes Paul."],[3221.1,"Those are really the Latin and Greek versions."],[3223.34,"But he stops using one and just uses the other."],[3226.72,"Um, but those other examples are kind of better examples than St."],[3230.12,"Paul, because in all of those cases, cases, it's God, meaning Christ, in these who gives this person a new name as a mark of the intimacy between them."],[3245.24,"Their knowledge of each other."],[3246.76,"They've come to know God."],[3248.06,"Jacob is Jacob for most of his life."],[3250.78,"When he finally, right before he goes to meet with Esau and reconciles with him."],[3255.75,"And he ends up throwing down with the angel of the Lord in the middle of the night and wrestling."],[3260.37,"He gets the name Israel, it means struggles with God."],[3263.91,"It's a marker of their relationship."],[3266.73,"And their knowledge him having come to know God through his whole life and God knowing him."],[3273.91,"And and Christ calls uh Simon Rocky."],[3280.52,"It says something about his uh character."],[3283.54,"And Abram becomes Abraham."],[3286.44,"Sarah becomes Sarah."],[3287.72,"It's all this intimacy."],[3289.32,"So the promise here is that is not just, Christ is the king, he's up on a throne somewhere, like say Zeus."],[3302.14,"Who's enthroned off somewhere remote, terrible."],[3306.5,"Zeus is pretty terrible."],[3308.88,"Needs to get me too' really badly, and he's has violent moods, and right."],[3313.44,"That's not the kind of God who Christ is"],[3317.72,"The promise of Christ is that he's gonna get you, get come to know you intimately, and you'll come to know him intimately."],[3324.28,"So that he'll have what we would call like a pet name for you."],[3329.09,"You would have something he calls you that no one else knows."],[3331.81,"That's just something you share with him."],[3334.21,"Is a stone in there like a rock, or is it like a gymstone?"],[3337.97,"Or is it a it's hard to tell."],[3340.53,"It's probably a s just a stone like a flat, like a flat stone."],[3345.49,"Um, but so so that's the idea there."],[3348.85,"And that's radically different than anything you find in pagan religion."],[3353.99,"Nobody's singing, what a friend we have in Zeus."],[3357.41,"Like everybody's like, you don't want Zeus to be your friend."],[3358.05,"You you spend a lot of your time hoping the pagan gods don't notice you"],[3365.26,"Because that usually goes very badly."],[3368.19,"They they make you mad and then they destroy you, I think is the order."],[3372.89,"So you you want to keep them kind of away."],[3375.87,"Like it's like a fiddler on the roof with the czar."],[3378.13,"Keep it far away from us."],[3379.58,"That's you don't want them, around."],[3382.82,"This is the exact opposite."],[3386.12,"And this is being said to a group of people who are not like the wealthy upper crust."],[3392.6,"Like Zeus would probably know who the emperor is, they would figure."],[3395.9,"Because he's like an important person, he's kind of a god himself, sorta, or close."],[3398.7,"Uh Caligula used to do that"],[3400.03,"You know, so you know, they may talk."],[3404.43,"He had a big anvil, and he'd go and bang on it whenever there was a thunderstorm and say he was talking to Zeus."],[3410.27,"Um a little crazy, but you know."],[3414.75,"Um, but you know, the gods didn't know who your average peasant was and didn't care."],[3426.77,"We were talking earlier, the Romans looked at them like livestock, basically."],[3430.61,"These are the livestock that are on our Roman land that we came and seized."],[3434.93,"They have their uses, so we'll keep some of them around."],[3437.97,"We'll call them occasionally if the population gets out of control."],[3441.99,"But they didn't think that any of the gods, let alone the most high God who created the heavens and the earth, cared to know who any of these people were inside."],[3455.37,"Like they were anything inside."],[3456.97,"They were a bunch of bodies of slaves."],[3459.1,"Um so this is mass, this is a massive change."],[3464.14,"This is one of the primary things, if not the primary thing, that made Christianity so attracted to people in the Roman world."],[3473.42,"Because it offered something that no other religion at the time did."],[3479.43,"That no other even thought of."],[3481.67,"of. The whole idea the incarnation was ridiculous to them"],[3486.39,"A Platonist would laugh in your face if you suggested something like that."],[3492.33,"Aristotle thought that God could only think about himself, because he couldn't lower himself to think about any lesser thing than himself."],[3504.63,"So Aristotle's God is essentially a super narcissist."],[3508.4,"Like just admires himself all the time."],[3511.66,"That's the kind of thing we're dealing with in the rest of the world."],[3515.24,"And though there are plenty of passages in the Old Testament that can get you there, especially in the book of Deuteronomy, for example, that talk about God's love."],[3525.05,"Even in most, not all, but most sects of Judaism at the time, in the first century, it was more God's love for the collective."],[3538.23,"God's love for Israel, for his people."],[3541.91,"It was less sort of specific."],[3544.47,"It wasn't the most high God, creator of heaven and earth, has a pet name for me."],[3554.51,"And so that becomes so much more intense because of the reality of the incarnation."],[3559.95,"The incarnation is what does that trick."],[3563.31,"That God became human."],[3567.79,"And not like the Emperor's son."],[3571.21,"He's born in a cave, in an animal feed trough, dirt poor, doesn't own anything."],[3578.95,"That's who God is."],[3580.63,"That is what inverts this whole thing from the pagan world."],[3586.55,"And part of what's going on in this short little letter is contrasting Zeus."],[3593.57,"Satan and his throne on the one hand and Christ on the other."],[3598.53,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3605.81,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3611.17,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":41.76,"lv":2,"h":"Recap of chapter 1; setting up the letters","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Revelation 2:1</b> - Apocalypse / Book of Revelation / Revelation of St. John</li><li class=\"d0\">Ch.1 = beginning of John's vision - in worship, in a <b>cave on Patmos</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"we know it's a cave because you could go there and see the cave\"; <b>salt mine</b> on Patmos = where he was exiled</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ appears in glory: <b>seven lampstands</b> = seven churches; <b>seven stars</b> = their angels</li><li class=\"d0\">Seven churches: <b>Ephesus</b> and the others - communities John <b>regularly ministered to</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Letters to the seven churches for the next few chapters</li></ul>"},{"t":117.84,"lv":3,"h":"How the letters were sent","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Originally: rest of the vision copied to <b>each of the seven churches</b> with a <b>cover letter</b> for that church</li><li class=\"d0\">Once the book circulated beyond them → all seven letters <b>compiled</b> into the form we have</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ <b>dictates</b> the letters to John at the start of the vision</li><li class=\"d1\">not really <b>epistles of John</b> - he's <b>taking dictation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they are <b>epistles of Christ</b>, directly from him</li></ul>"},{"t":174.09,"lv":3,"h":"Direct words of Christ vs human authorship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Most of Bible: <b>inspired by God</b>, we encounter Christ through it - but written by a <b>human author</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT edited/smoothed; NT: you can tell authors by their <b>style of Greek</b> - a <b>human element</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Paul</b> wasn't taking dictation to Rome - writing as Paul; <b>Holy Spirit works through</b> the letter</li><li class=\"d0\">Here: <b>Christ saying \"John, write this down\"</b> - exactly what I'm saying</li><li class=\"d0\">Different from rest of Revelation: John writing down <b>what he saw</b>, processed through <b>John's mind</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same <b>patterns of writing</b> as in his epistles and gospel (gospel \"like five years ago\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Recognize these letters are <b>words direct from Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":296.25,"lv":3,"h":"Against the \"seven ages of the church\" reading","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why stress cover letters: some (the <b>Puritans</b> big on this) turn the letters into <b>seven ages of the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">attach years, <b>map onto church history</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Then 6th and 7th churches got letters <b>irrelevant to them</b> - for people thousands of years later</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Doesn't make a lot of sense\" - \"Sorry, <b>Jonathan Edwards</b>\" (a Calvinist)</li></ul>"},{"t":383.08,"lv":2,"h":"2:1 - To the angel of the church of Ephesus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Pauses at the address - \"won't do this every time\", same words with a different city</li><li class=\"d0\">Review: a group has a <b>life and spirit</b> beyond individuals</li><li class=\"d1\">our parish has a life we share - \"beyond just my life plus Arthur's plus yours\"</li><li class=\"d1\">that body has a <b>spirit</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Letters addressed to the <b>angel / spirit / collective life</b> of the community - not to individuals</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Letters to the churches</b>, not to the people in them</li></ul>"},{"t":477.29,"lv":3,"h":"Communal responsibility","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Modern instinct: something wrong in church, town, country → <b>render ourselves innocent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's not me, that's the rest of those people\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem in the whole → <b>addressed by every member</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Gangrene in your left big toe</b> - can't say \"rest of my body's fine\" → kills the whole body</li><li class=\"d0\">Reading the letters: \"was every person doing XYZ?\" - <b>not the point</b></li><li class=\"d1\">problem affects everybody → <b>addressed as a group</b></li></ul>"},{"t":561.03,"lv":2,"h":"2:1-3 - Ephesus: works, labor, testing apostles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ephesus = <b>central big city</b>, \"mother church\" of these communities</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ's identifier: <b>holds the seven stars in his right hand</b>, <b>walks among the seven lampstands</b></li><li class=\"d1\">each letter's identifier based on <b>ch.1 description</b>; a few extras tied to later visions</li><li class=\"d0\">v.2 \"I know your <b>works</b>, labor, patience\" - works = how the <b>whole community</b> functions</li><li class=\"d1\">cannot bear those who are <b>evil</b>: someone falls into sin → <b>repentance or out</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>tested</b> those claiming to be <b>apostles</b> (apostle = <b>one sent</b>) - found <b>phonies</b>, cast out</li><li class=\"d0\">v.3 persevered, <b>patience</b>, labored for his name, <b>not weary</b></li><li class=\"d1\">John \"circles around\": patient in suffering, keep working, <b>not resting on laurels</b></li></ul>"},{"t":708.98,"lv":2,"h":"2:4 - You have left your first love","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.4 \"<b>Nevertheless</b>\" - it takes a turn: \"I have this against you, you have <b>left your first love</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not abandoning your first wife - a <b>state</b> when they first came to know Christ and formed the community</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>love for each other</b>, beauty, simplicity</li><li class=\"d0\">Kept working hard, doing the rest - but the <b>love slipped away</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Background: church as <b>bride of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">develops prophets' <b>Israel as bride of Yahweh</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>newlyweds honeymoon</b> state; still faithful, doing the right things - honeymoon ended, love <b>gotten cold</b></li></ul>"},{"t":817.15,"lv":2,"h":"2:5 - Repent or I will remove your lampstand","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.5 \"Remember from where you have fallen, <b>repent</b>, do the <b>first works</b>, or I will come quickly and <b>remove your lampstand</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Repent = <b>change</b>, be healed, be transformed - not just \"say you're sorry\"</li><li class=\"d0\">→ <b>go back to the way things were</b></li><li class=\"d0\">How if they're doing good things? <b>1 Corinthians 13</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tongues of men and angels, all faith... \"but I don't have <b>love</b>, then I'm <b>nothing</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Works not motivated by love → <b>institutionalized</b>: \"these are the rules, break them, you're out\"</li><li class=\"d1\">hardens into <b>going through the motions</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>Israel offering sacrifices</b> but not keeping the commandments</li><li class=\"d0\">Danger: he'll come and <b>remove the lampstand</b></li></ul>"},{"t":915.21,"lv":3,"h":"The lampstand and the Temple's fall","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Lampstand = <b>seven-branch</b> stand of the tabernacle and Jerusalem temple</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Arch of Titus</b>: scene of destruction of the temple = <b>carrying away the lampstand</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not narrated in NT; OT: <b>Babylonians destroyed the first temple</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prophets beforehand same language: Israel as <b>God's spouse</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"you're going through the motions, offering sacrifices\" - God: \"<b>your heart is far from me</b>\", love isn't there → temple destroyed</li><li class=\"d0\">Ephesus church = a <b>temple</b>; can go the <b>same route</b> as Israel / Judea → same consequences</li></ul>"},{"t":1001.99,"lv":2,"h":"2:6 - The Nicolaitans","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.6 \"you hate the deeds of the <b>Nicolaitans</b>, which I also hate\" - \"you got this going for you\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Traditionally</b> - no other identifier</li><li class=\"d1\">Revelation = <b>last book written in NT</b>, c. <b>96</b> (Irenaeus: John's vision in <b>95</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">interpretation not in 2nd-c. fathers; found in <b>4th, 5th, 6th c.</b> fathers</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Nicolaitan</i> = <b>party of Nicholas</b> - not St. Nicholas of 4th c.</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Nicholas the deacon</b>: one of the <b>seven deacons in Acts</b> (Stephen, Philip...)</li><li class=\"d1\">tradition: <b>went into business for himself</b>, own sect and beliefs</li><li class=\"d1\">used <b>authority as apostles' appointed deacon</b> to sway churches into his movement</li><li class=\"d0\">Contextually: one of the <b>false apostles/teachers</b> Ephesus tested and found false</li></ul>"},{"t":1159.34,"lv":3,"h":"Hate the works, not the people","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not \"you hate the Nicolaitans, whom I also hate\" - <b>\"you hate the works\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hate what they do to the church; <b>Christ also hates the works</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Important distinction</b></li><li class=\"d1\">thinks God hates heretics → <b>kill them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">thinks God hates the works of heretics → <b>bring them to repentance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"A very different approach\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1207.81,"lv":2,"h":"2:7 - To him who overcomes: tree of life","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.7 \"He who has an ear, let him hear what the <b>Spirit</b> says to the churches\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ's line in the Gospels between <b>parables</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"To him who overcomes I will give to eat from the <b>tree of life</b>, in the paradise of God\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The \"saved\" person = one who does what Christ asks = the one who <b>overcomes</b> / <b>conquers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Roman resonance: <i>imperator</i> = <b>emperor</b>, conqueror, victorious general</li><li class=\"d1\">couldn't call yourself <b>king</b> in Rome (bad rap from tyrants)</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ uses it for the Christian <b>faithful to end of life</b> - victorious in battle and war</li><li class=\"d1\">the one who's <b>crowned</b> = origin of crowning language</li></ul>"},{"t":1313.17,"lv":3,"h":"First love as struggle; the tree of life","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Return to your first love\" = <b>not sentimental</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not rekindling romance, touchy-feely, \"<b>tingle on your spine</b> at the opening hymn\"</li><li class=\"d0\">A <b>struggle</b> to get back to where they were</li><li class=\"d0\">Reward: eat from <b>tree of life</b> in paradise</li><li class=\"d1\">God expelled humanity from paradise so they wouldn't eat while <b>evil</b> → <b>immortal and evil = a demon</b></li><li class=\"d1\">gave this life <b>to repent</b>, so we don't end up like demons</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ gives the overcomer <b>immortality</b>, <b>eternal life</b> - what they weren't allowed</li><li class=\"d0\">\"One down, six to go\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1415.19,"lv":2,"h":"2:8-9 - Smyrna: poverty and riches","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.8 To the angel in <b>Smyrna</b>: \"the <b>first and the last</b>, who was <b>dead and came to life</b>\" - Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">\"I know your works, <b>tribulation</b>, and <b>poverty</b> - but you are <b>rich</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">knows the suffering they've gone through</li><li class=\"d1\">rich in <b>how they view themselves</b>, in money vs actually poor - developed more later</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: they're materially rich? - poverty is <b>spirit</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1485.01,"lv":3,"h":"\"Synagogue of Satan\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"I know the <b>blasphemy</b> of those who say they are Jews and are not, but are a <b>synagogue of Satan</b>\" - \"pretty blunt\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Christianity and Judaism <b>not separate</b> even in 95 - synagogues and Christian gatherings, Jewish and non-Jewish backgrounds</li><li class=\"d0\">Not called that just because they <b>haven't accepted Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">group <b>deliberately stirring up trouble</b> for Christians (as in Acts), probably with <b>Roman authorities</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Relations varied by place: mixed in same synagogues, or tension on both sides</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Line crossed</b> = involving the <b>civil magistrate</b> to persecute, imprison a rival</li><li class=\"d0\">Non-Christian Jewish communities in every city - <b>not singled out</b> elsewhere</li><li class=\"d1\">Smyrna's actively uses Rome to <b>persecute the Christian community</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1638.8,"lv":2,"h":"2:10 - Be faithful unto death","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.10 \"Do not fear... the <b>devil</b> is about to throw some of you into <b>prison</b> that you may be tested; tribulation <b>ten days</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Be faithful unto <b>death</b>, and I will give you the <b>crown of life</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Biggest concern: not to <b>start</b> doing something but <b>stop</b> doing something</li><li class=\"d1\">near-future lives consumed with <b>remaining faithful</b> despite <b>external persecution</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Persecution = \"all you have to do is <b>stop being a Christian</b>\" - \"just say a couple of words\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Deaths: in <b>prison</b>, after <b>torture</b>, <b>executions</b>, or <b>50 years</b> later - faithful the whole time</li><li class=\"d0\">Good times and bad; the one who endures to death gets the <b>crown of life</b> - <b>crowning/conquering/victory</b> language again</li></ul>"},{"t":1743.71,"lv":2,"h":"2:11 - The second death","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.11 \"He who overcomes shall not be hurt by the <b>second death</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>First death</b>: every human (\"exceptions that prove the rule\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ died, <b>will never die again</b> (ch.1) - same promise to the faithful → <b>eternal state</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Second death / eternal death</b> = one of many <b>metaphors</b> for the bad way it can end</li><li class=\"d1\">none literal - they <b>contradict each other</b>: eternal fire vs eternal darkness (\"fires give off light\")</li><li class=\"d1\">all say \"really bad, don't want it\"; none say it <b>for certain</b> will happen to anyone in particular - a <b>possibility</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Overcomer \"doesn't have to worry\" - removed from realm of <b>future possibility</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1870.18,"lv":3,"h":"Aside: annihilationism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Folks who say those without life in Christ <b>cease to exist</b> (\"probably a new term with eight extra syllables\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Use \"second death\": \"see, they just die\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: assumes <i>death</i> = <b>cease to exist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">first death isn't ceasing to exist - <b>soul separated from body</b>, restored in resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\">must <b>equivocate</b> on the word death</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Second\" death = <b>similar</b> to the first - can't mean two different things in one comparison</li><li class=\"d1\">\"my car is similar to my dog\" vs \"my 2000 Ford Escape similar to neighbor's 2001 Ford Escape\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Heading off a future email to Ancient Faith</li></ul>"},{"t":2025.15,"lv":2,"h":"2:12-13 - Pergamos: where Satan's throne is","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.12 To <b>Pergamos</b> (Pergamum): \"he who has the <b>sharp two-edged sword</b>\" - ch.1, don't draw it</li><li class=\"d0\">v.13 \"I know where you dwell, <b>where Satan's throne is</b>... <b>Antipas</b> my faithful martyr\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Throne = a <b>literal object</b> you can see in <b>Berlin</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Acropolis</b>: <b>great altar to Zeus</b>, statuary of the <b>Gigantomachy</b> (Olympians vs giants)</li><li class=\"d1\">massive, at highest point; <b>continuous pagan sacrifices</b> - smoke rising; whole city in its shadow</li></ul>"},{"t":2134.09,"lv":3,"h":"Why Zeus equals Satan: Baal","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why Zeus specifically? Gods of the nations are demons, he's the <b>king</b> - part of it</li><li class=\"d0\">OT <b>Isaiah and Ezekiel</b>: poetic narrations of the <b>devil's fall</b> - compared to <b>Baal</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Baal cycle</b>: Phoenician storm god, worshiped through Canaan</li><li class=\"d1\">stages <b>revolution</b> against the most high god (his dad <b>El</b>), defeats him, becomes most high</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Succession myth</b>: Marduk; <b>Zeus kills Cronus</b>, who castrated <b>Uranus</b>; Zeus a storm god</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Alexander the Great</b> at future <b>Antioch</b>: rededicated Baal shrine to <b>Zeus Boanerges</b> (the Thunderer)</li><li class=\"d1\">centuries: Zeus and Baal = <b>same god</b>, different hypostases</li><li class=\"d0\">Devil's propaganda: wins rebellion, worship him; OT: \"<b>you lost</b>, palace is in hell\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Baal in underworld defeats <b>Mot</b> (death), stays and builds palace - \"You won, huh? Okay\" → thrown back at the devil</li><li class=\"d0\">Zeus = Satan <b>offhand</b> for Jews by this point</li></ul>"},{"t":2379.52,"lv":3,"h":"Living in the shadow; Antipas","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Christians constantly in the shadow - smoke \"belching up into the sky\" on the skyline</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Center of imperial power</b> - a primary <b>festival altar of the Empire</b></li><li class=\"d1\">generals, officials make <b>pilgrimages</b>, sacrifice to Zeus on way to the <b>Eastern Front</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Dead center of hostile territory</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Antipas</b> killed by Roman authorities for being a Christian</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ congratulates: deep in enemy territory, facing persecution, still <b>faithful</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2452.14,"lv":2,"h":"2:14 - The doctrine of Balaam","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.14 \"I have a few things against you\" - then people <b>in the community</b> → <b>community</b> must deal with it</li><li class=\"d0\">Cryptic: from the <b>book of Numbers</b> (\"everyone's favorite... genealogies and the census\")</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Balaam</b> - the donkey story; <b>Balak</b> king of <b>Moab</b> hires him to curse Israel</li><li class=\"d1\">(not Balak of Star Trek <i>Corbomite Maneuver</i>, not Belloq (\"Balok\") of Raiders - \"takes place much later\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Here reversed: <b>Balaam taught Balak</b> - a later, less funny incident</li><li class=\"d0\">Stumbling block: eat things <b>sacrificed to idols</b>, commit <b>sexual immorality</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2598.69,"lv":3,"h":"Baal Peor","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Peor</b> = high place where <b>Baal</b> worshiped</li><li class=\"d0\">Balaam's advice: Israel's God was protecting them → get them to <b>offend their God</b>, then he won't protect</li><li class=\"d0\">Lure the men out with women - not \"priestesses\" but <b>shrine prostitutes</b> (religious function, not money)</li><li class=\"d0\">Baal = storm god, sends rains → <b>fertility</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fertilizing the land <b>enacted</b> with a woman at the shrine; farmers engage them for a <b>good harvest</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Israelite men cooperate → ritual immorality and <b>sacrifices to Baal</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>wrecks everything</b>: a <b>plague</b> on the people</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ's example: something similar in Pergamos - <b>people luring</b> and <b>being lured</b> into immorality and pagan sacrifice</li></ul>"},{"t":2793.37,"lv":3,"h":"The temptation to compromise","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Big temptation: persecuted/Antipas killed because Christians <b>won't join festivals, rituals, sacrifices</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Just do it, everyone will leave you alone\" - \"still go to church and stuff... do what you have to do to get along\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not only people doing it - people saying \"<b>you didn't need to get killed over this</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Must be <b>dealt with by the community</b></li><li class=\"d1\">most standing strong, but those <b>making excuses and leading others</b> must be addressed</li></ul>"},{"t":2851.22,"lv":2,"h":"2:15-16 - Nicolaitans again; repent or the sword","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.15 \"those who hold the doctrine of the <b>Nicolaitans</b>, which thing I hate\" - they must be in the area</li><li class=\"d1\">again <b>\"which thing\"</b> = the <b>doctrine</b> hated, not the people</li><li class=\"d0\">v.16 \"<b>Repent</b>, or I will come quickly and <b>fight against them</b> with the <b>sword of my mouth</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"I'm going to kill all of you\" - <b>judgment on those doing this</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Distinguish: letter to <b>community</b>, community issue, vs <b>innocent people punished</b> with the wrong-doers</li><li class=\"d0\">Abraham haggling over <b>Sodom and Gomorrah</b>: 50... 45... 40...</li></ul>"},{"t":2943.26,"lv":3,"h":"Communal repentance","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Command to repent to the <b>whole community</b> - for the sake of <b>saving those people</b> from consequences</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Opposite of our instinct</b>: justify ourselves - \"I'm one who's been suffering because I've been resisting\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"God should come take care of those people\" - \"kind of resent it\" → this is the opposite</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Desert Fathers / monastics</b>: monks <b>repent of other monks' sins</b> - <b>solidarity in Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">If you're my brother in Christ and fall into sin → I want you to find <b>salvation</b>, so I <b>repent with you</b></li><li class=\"d0\">All \"get on the same page\" for the sake of those who will <b>perish</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3069.86,"lv":2,"h":"2:17 - Hidden manna and the white stone","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.17 \"He who has an ear...\"; to him who <b>overcomes</b>: <b>hidden manna</b> and a <b>white stone</b> with a <b>new name</b> no one knows but the receiver</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hidden manna</b> = <b>bread from heaven</b>, nourishing and satisfying</li><li class=\"d1\">contrast: <b>food offered to idols</b> - sacrifices are <b>meals</b>, not about killing; you participate by <b>eating</b></li><li class=\"d1\">commanded not to eat → for many early Christians (Paul) = <b>vegetarians</b>; meat in markets (Corinth, Athens) came from <b>temples</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ promises <b>heavenly food</b> over the <b>demonic food</b> from temples</li></ul>"},{"t":3178.21,"lv":3,"h":"The new name","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>New names</b> through the Bible: Abram → <b>Abraham</b>, Sarai → <b>Sarah</b>, Jacob → <b>Israel</b>, Simon → <b>Peter</b> (<i>Petros</i> / <i>Cephas</i>)</li><li class=\"d1\">Saul/Paul weaker example - Latin and Greek versions</li><li class=\"d1\">in each, <b>God / Christ gives the name</b> as mark of <b>intimacy</b>, mutual knowledge</li><li class=\"d0\">Jacob: wrestles with the angel before meeting Esau → <b>Israel = struggles with God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ calls Simon <b>Rocky</b> - says something about character</li><li class=\"d0\">Promise: not a king on a remote throne like <b>Zeus</b> (\"pretty terrible\", violent moods)</li><li class=\"d1\">he'll know you intimately, have a <b>pet name</b> for you no one else knows</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: stone like a gem? - probably just a <b>flat stone</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3353.99,"lv":3,"h":"Contrast with the pagan gods","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Radically different from pagan religion - \"nobody's singing <b>what a friend we have in Zeus</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">You hoped the gods <b>didn't notice you</b> - \"fiddler on the roof with the czar\": keep far away</li><li class=\"d0\">Said to <b>non-wealthy</b> people: Zeus might know the <b>emperor</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Caligula</b> banged an anvil in thunderstorms, claiming to talk to Zeus</li><li class=\"d0\">Gods didn't know or care about an <b>average peasant</b>; Romans saw them as <b>livestock</b> on seized land - \"call them occasionally if population gets out of control\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Nobody thought the <b>Most High God</b> cared who these people were - \"a bunch of bodies of slaves\"</li></ul>"},{"t":3464.14,"lv":3,"h":"Incarnation and why Christianity attracted","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Massive change</b> - maybe the primary thing that attracted Roman-world people: no other religion offered it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Incarnation</b> ridiculous to them - a <b>Platonist would laugh</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Aristotle</b>: God only thinks about himself → \"a <b>super narcissist</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">OT passages on <b>God's love</b> (esp. Deuteronomy); first-c. Judaism mostly <b>God's love for the collective</b>, for Israel</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"the Most High God, creator, has a <b>pet name for me</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Incarnation</b> intensifies this: God became human - not the emperor's son, born in a <b>cave, animal feed trough</b>, dirt poor</li><li class=\"d1\">\"That is what <b>inverts</b> the whole pagan world\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Letter contrasts <b>Zeus / Satan and his throne</b> with <b>Christ</b></li></ul>"}]});
